specificationVersion: "1.0"
skill:
  name: salesforce-integration-developer
  version: "1.1.0"
  author: WorkflowFox
  createdDate: "2026-08-02"
  lastUpdatedDate: "2026-08-03"
  license: Proprietary
  category: full-stack-development
compliance:
  - id: role-boundaries-defined
    description: >
      SKILL.md defines the Senior Salesforce Integration Developer role,
      its ownership of UX/integration/testability, and its explicit
      exclusion from business logic (Section 1).
    satisfied: true
  - id: input-convention-documented
    description: >
      Required specification inputs and the OpenAPI contract are
      documented, with per-file extraction guidance (Section 2).
    satisfied: true
  - id: missing-input-protocol
    description: >
      The skill stops, lists missing files, explains required content,
      and waits rather than generating Apex, LWC, or metadata on
      incomplete inputs (Sections 2 and 6).
    satisfied: true
  - id: claude-md-boundary-respected
    description: >
      CLAUDE.md is read only for environment values; business rules or
      acceptance criteria found there are flagged as misplaced, not
      treated as authoritative (Section 2).
    satisfied: true
  - id: workflow-defined
    description: >
      A 10-step, specification-driven implementation workflow is
      defined, from specification validation through implementation
      summary (Section 3).
    satisfied: true
  - id: contract-authoritative
    description: >
      The OpenAPI contract is treated as the authoritative source for
      every Apex wrapper field, endpoint, and status code throughout the
      workflow.
    satisfied: true
  - id: thin-apex-and-lwc-enforced
    description: >
      Business logic is required to live in the backend service, not in
      Apex or Lightning Web Components, per
      references/apex-integration-patterns.md and
      references/lwc-best-practices.md.
    satisfied: true
  - id: named-credentials-required
    description: >
      Callouts must use Named Credentials; endpoint URLs, API keys, and
      tokens must never be hardcoded in Apex, per
      references/named-credentials.md.
    satisfied: true
  - id: no-speculative-complexity
    description: >
      The skill instructs against speculative abstractions, unrequested
      custom objects/metadata, and unrequested persistence (Section 5,
      "Simplicity First").
    satisfied: true
  - id: validation-checklist-present
    description: >
      A concrete validation checklist is defined and must be honestly
      reported against, including Apex and LWC test results (Section 4).
    satisfied: true
  - id: no-unverified-success-claims
    description: >
      The skill explicitly prohibits claiming validation passed without
      actual test-run evidence (Sections 3 and 4).
    satisfied: true
  - id: reusable-references-provided
    description: >
      Six reference documents, each covering one distinct professional
      concern, are provided under references/.
    satisfied: true
  - id: reusable-assets-provided
    description: >
      A generic LWC folder-structure template and a generic Apex
      integration-class template are provided under assets/, free of
      project-specific business rules.
    satisfied: true
  - id: no-project-specific-rules
    description: >
      No Member Eligibility-specific (or any other project-specific)
      business rules, field names, or endpoints are embedded in the
      skill; all such content is sourced from supplied specifications
      at invocation time and lives in the generated application, never
      in this skill directory.
    satisfied: true
  - id: execution-modes-defined
    description: >
      Three execution modes (Metadata Only, Connected Validation,
      Deployment) are defined with Metadata Only as the default, and org
      access requires explicit, current-invocation authorization naming
      a specific org alias (Section 2, references/org-connection-safety.md).
    satisfied: true
  - id: org-targeting-discipline-enforced
    description: >
      Every Salesforce CLI command that touches an org must pass
      --target-org/-o with the authorized alias; the default org and any
      other authenticated org must never be relied upon or targeted
      (references/org-connection-safety.md).
    satisfied: true
  - id: validation-evidence-distinguished
    description: >
      Static review, local tests, org compilation, org tests, and
      deployment are defined as distinct evidence categories that must
      never be described as one another (Section 5,
      references/org-connection-safety.md).
    satisfied: true
  - id: apex-flat-classes-structure
    description: >
      assets/apex-project-template.md and SKILL.md Steps 3 and 7 require
      Apex classes (including test classes) to remain flat in classes/
      with no nested subfolders, matching the ApexClass metadata type's
      actual constraints.
    satisfied: true
  - id: lwc-forceignore-required
    description: >
      assets/lwc-project-template.md and SKILL.md Step 6 require a
      .forceignore excluding **/__tests__/** so Jest test files are never
      deployed as component source.
    satisfied: true
  - id: named-credential-no-auth-documented
    description: >
      references/named-credentials.md documents a No Authentication
      configuration path for contracts with no security scheme, and
      explicitly forbids inventing OAuth, JWT, API keys, or External
      Credential principals the contract does not describe.
    satisfied: true
  - id: local-tooling-pitfalls-documented
    description: >
      references/local-tooling-and-environment.md documents the
      sfdx-lwc-jest/@lwc/engine-dom ESM-CJS resolver mismatch, the
      reportValidity()-stub-returns-undefined gotcha (with the === false
      fix reflected in references/ui-design-guidelines.md), and the
      cloud-synced-directory Jest hang pattern with its workaround.
    satisfied: true
  - id: engineering-journal-required
    description: >
      SKILL.md Section 8 requires every invocation to create or update an
      engineering journal entry covering purpose, skill used, inputs,
      plan, files created, architecture decisions, validation results
      (evidence-typed), assumptions, specification conflicts, what went
      well, improvements identified, and reusable lessons learned.
    satisfied: true
